      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"Stress-induced anisotropy\"—okay, that sounds like exactly the kind of technical nuance the committee would need to hear to move past the initial alarm. \n\nBut I need to be really careful here. If they pull the raw sensor data and see those pore-pressure spikes, they're going to argue that anisotropy doesn't excuse a high risk of liquefaction. I need to be able to show them that the numbers themselves, when interpreted through this \"anisotropy\" lens, actually fall within the safe zones.\n\nCan you give me the specific values? I need the actual pore-pressure numbers and the density readings from the report that support this. If I can show them that, say, the pressure is only at 0.5 bar or that the density is well within the expected range for this kind of adaptive soil, then I can present this with a lot more confidence. What are the actual figures we're working with?",
